Starting from the original failing replicate8 lesion:
- add2 alone rescues only levels14..16.
- remove6 alone rescues no level.
- add2+remove6 rescues levels10..13 only.
- neutral add10, remove14, and add10+remove14 rescue no level.
The original replicate8 anchor failed and full partner6 anchor rescued at every level8..16.
Interpretation: cell2/cell6 are necessary to preserve the full partner6 rescue basin (C24) but are not globally sufficient to create rescue from the replicate8 basin. Repair is path- and pressure-dependent.
Critically, prior C22 already established a distinct one-swap repair 38->42 that rescues replicate8 at every level8..16 while retaining replicate8's cell2/cell6 status. Therefore at least two geometrically distinct rescue routes exist.
Plain speak: there is not one universal recipe for repair. The partner6-style repair needs cell2 in and cell6 out, but another known repair works without making those changes.
Next: start from the universally rescuing 38->42 state and add the partner-basin signatures (add2 and/or remove6) with matched controls to test whether the two repair geometries are compatible or mutually interfering.
